const READING_DEEP_DIVE={1:{
1:[
['Start with the question','The handbook treats data analysis as more than manipulating numbers. The useful starting point is the purpose of the work: what does the organisation need to understand, decide or improve? Once that purpose is clear, the analyst can turn it into specific questions. Those questions then influence what evidence matters. This prevents a common beginner mistake: opening a dataset first and searching for an interesting chart without knowing what the analysis is supposed to accomplish.'],
['Data is raw material','A data value can be real and still be incomplete as evidence. A number, word, picture or record becomes more useful when the analyst knows what it represents, where it came from, when it was collected and why it was collected. Think of raw data as material that still needs to be organised and interpreted. The goal is not to decorate the material with charts; the goal is to turn it into information that can support a decision.'],
['Information needs context','Suppose two reports both contain the value 80. One might mean 80 customers; another might mean 80 percent. Even the same measure can mean different things if the time period or population changes. Context therefore protects the analyst from false comparisons. Before calculating, ask: What is the unit? What is the population? What period does it cover? What does each category mean?'],
['Patterns are a starting point','Sorting, grouping and visualising data can expose a pattern. That is useful, but a pattern is not automatically an explanation. If one month is unusually high, the responsible next step is to investigate the circumstances around that month and check whether the evidence supports a broader claim. Good analysts separate observation from interpretation. They can say what the data shows without pretending that the cause is already known.'],
['Your first professional habit','A strong beginner habit is to write the question before writing the conclusion. Keep the evidence that supports your statement visible, record important assumptions, and make the final explanation understandable to someone who did not perform the analysis. These habits become more important as datasets become larger and methods become more technical.']
],
2:[
['From data to information','The handbook describes data as facts from which conclusions may be drawn, while emphasising that raw values need to be gathered and sorted before they can be interpreted. This distinction matters in practice. A spreadsheet full of values is not automatically an answer. An analyst has to connect observations to definitions, questions and context. The same dataset can support different analyses depending on the purpose for which it is being used.'],
['Context changes interpretation','Imagine a report saying that 70 people attended an event. Without a date, location or definition of attendance, the statement is incomplete. Was it 70 unique people or 70 visits? Was the event one hour or one week? Context determines which comparisons are legitimate. This is why an analyst checks labels, units, dates, categories and the population represented before making a finding.'],
['Visuals help you see','A visual display can give you a quick sense of how values differ. A large gap, cluster or unusual observation can become easier to notice than it would be in a long table. But the handbook treats visual displays as a foundation for analysis, not a substitute for it. The analyst still needs to ask what the pattern means, whether the data is appropriate for the comparison, and what additional evidence is needed.'],
['Observation versus finding','Consider the statement: “Region A has the highest value.” That is an observation if it accurately describes the dataset. A stronger finding would explain what that observation means in relation to the original question and what the available evidence can actually support. The difference is important because analysts are often tempted to turn an interesting observation into a causal story too quickly.'],
['A disciplined conclusion','A disciplined analyst uses language that matches the strength of the evidence. If the dataset shows a difference, report the difference. If several pieces of evidence support an interpretation, explain the connection. If uncertainty remains, say so. Data analysis becomes credible when the analyst does not claim more than the evidence can carry.']
],
3:[
['Questions drive the work','The handbook places specific questions near the beginning of the analysis process because questions guide decisions about data collection and analytical procedures. A broad goal such as “understand customer experience” is difficult to analyse directly. A more useful question might ask which service issues customers mention most often during a defined period. The second question gives the analyst something concrete to investigate.'],
['Choose evidence deliberately','Once a question exists, decide what evidence can answer it. Do not automatically collect everything available. Consider whether the evidence is relevant, available and of sufficient quality for the question. The handbook also emphasises that data availability matters: an ideal measure is not useful if the system never collected it. Analysts therefore work with both the question and the actual evidence they can obtain.'],
['Keep the trail visible','A useful analysis can be reconstructed. Record where the data came from, what period it covers, what categories mean and what decisions were made during preparation. This is especially important when more than one person works on the analysis. Clear records help another person understand what was done and reduce the chance that an unexplained spreadsheet change becomes part of the final result.'],
['Explain the evidence','The final reader should not have to reverse-engineer the analysis. State the purpose, the question, the important evidence, the finding and the interpretation in a logical order. Tables and charts should support the narrative rather than contradict it. The handbook also recommends describing methodology so readers understand how the result was produced.'],
['A practical analyst loop','For beginner work, use a repeatable loop: define the purpose, write the question, identify the evidence, inspect it, choose an appropriate method, describe the result, interpret carefully, and review the process. This turns analysis from random spreadsheet activity into an organised practice.']
],
4:[
['Numbers and words','Real datasets can contain numbers, words, pictures, maps and other observations. Two foundational categories are quantitative and qualitative evidence. Quantitative evidence is represented numerically, while qualitative evidence commonly appears as words or narrative descriptions. The distinction is useful because different kinds of evidence have different strengths and require different ways of working with them.'],
['Quantitative does not mean automatically better','A numerical value can be precise and still answer the wrong question. Likewise, a narrative response can reveal an issue that a count cannot explain. The handbook explicitly treats the qualitative-versus-quantitative debate as less useful than choosing evidence that fits the purpose. In some situations, combining both types can provide a more credible picture.'],
['Read the collection method','An analyst should ask how an observation was produced. A closed survey item may produce a numerical category or count. An open-ended response may produce narrative evidence. An interview transcript may reveal themes and recurring ideas. Understanding collection helps prevent the analyst from treating every field in a spreadsheet as if it were the same kind of measurement.'],
['Beware numeric labels','Numbers are not automatically measurements. If a survey uses 1 for “Yes” and 2 for “No,” the values are category labels. Averaging those labels would not create a meaningful “average yes/no” result. Classification must come before calculation. The analyst has to understand what the number represents before deciding what operations are legitimate.'],
['Mixed evidence needs discipline','When a project contains both numerical and narrative evidence, preserve the role of each. Numerical evidence can show amounts, counts or distributions; narrative evidence can provide descriptions and context. Combining them should strengthen the answer to the question, not flatten every kind of evidence into one artificial score.']
],
5:[
['Bring the week together','By the end of the first week, the key habit is to think before calculating. Data analysis begins with purpose and questions. Evidence must be understood in context and classified correctly. Patterns can guide investigation, but they are not automatically explanations. These ideas form the foundation for later procedures such as summary measures, trend analysis and qualitative methods.'],
['A complete mini-analysis','Imagine a small business has weekly sales, customer counts and written customer comments. A useful analysis might begin by asking whether sales changed across the period and what customer comments suggest about the service. The sales and customer counts are numerical evidence; the comments are qualitative evidence. The analyst can examine the numbers for patterns while separately examining the comments for recurring themes.'],
['Choose methods after questions','A chart may help compare weekly sales. A trend analysis may help examine change across time. Narrative responses may need coding or theme identification. The method follows the question and evidence; it should not be chosen simply because it looks advanced. This principle becomes increasingly important as the course moves into more formal analysis procedures.'],
['Observation, interpretation, recommendation','Keep three things separate. Observation describes what the evidence shows. Interpretation considers what that result may mean in relation to the question. Recommendation describes an action that follows from the findings. Mixing these steps can cause an analyst to present an opinion as though it were a measured result.'],
['Ready for assessment','Before the graded quiz, you should be able to explain the difference between data and information, distinguish quantitative and qualitative evidence, explain why context matters, identify why purpose and questions come first, and describe why a visible pattern is not automatically a causal conclusion. If you can explain those ideas without copying the wording from the reading, you are ready for the assessment.']
]}};

